THE SKY WAS MINED

Duration: 25s
Aspect: 9:16 cinematic
Style: Hyper-kinetic industrial sci-fi catastrophe
Tone: Ancient orbital graveyard / delayed apocalypse / mass-driver collapse

Premise

An abandoned ring of fortress-cities around a living planet begins to fail when buried asteroids in the defense lattice are remotely ignited. Entire mineral bodies light from the inside and drift between ruined megastructures, turning orbit itself into a falling minefield.

Visual identity
Rusted, cathedral-like orbital fortresses
Dense layered industrial architecture
Glowing asteroids with internal magma fractures
Dust plumes, debris clouds, ember trails
Distant planet filling background with cloud bands and curvature
Tiny defensive craft scattering like insects between impossible-scale objects
25s escalation arc

0.00–0.05

Start already deep inside the orbital ruin field.
Camera racing low through dense floating megastructures.
A huge asteroid ahead is dark 

0.05–0.09

Multiple asteroid bodies around frame start glowing one after another like a chain reaction.
Not explosions yet — pressure awakening inside rock.
Small defense craft streak across frame trying to flee the corridor.

0.09–0.13

One burning asteroid drifts dangerously close to a needle-like fortress spine.
Near miss. Shockwave dust rolls over the structure.
Chunks shear off and tumble toward camera.

0.13–0.17

Camera whip-dives downward through debris.
In the far background, another asteroid detonates partially, not fully destroying itself — just cracking open into a brighter molten core.
The orbital cityscape starts to collapse in layers.

0.17–0.21

Full chaos.
Burning fragments rain through the architecture.
Tiny ships thread between falling wreckage.
The planet behind grows larger as the whole field seems to be sinking inward.

0.21–0.25

Final acceleration shot: camera bursts out of the densest debris channel and reveals the horrifying truth — this is not one accident, but an entire mined orbital belt igniting across the horizon.
Multiple asteroids flare alive across space at once.
Cut on impact-bass before resolution.

Camera law
Always moving forward
Tight passes near structure edges
No static admiration shots
Camera behaves like it is trying to survive the corridor
Occasional whip-pans caused by debris or near-collision
Heavy parallax from foreground wreckage and towers
Scale revealed by tiny ships crossing frame for less than 0.5s
Sound
Music
Industrial war score + dark-electro pulse
Low brass drones
Huge sub impacts
Metallic percussion, slow but punishing
No heroic melody
SFX
Deep rock stress
Distant concussive thumps in vacuum-stylized cinematic mix
Debris shearing past camera
Fortress hull groans
Molten crackling textures
Engine scream from tiny fleeing craft

